WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.420
(bright music)

00:00:01.420 --> 00:00:02.253
- Give me a hug.

00:00:02.253 --> 00:00:03.086
Give me a hug.

00:00:03.086 --> 00:00:04.620
Yes, I know. Good girl.

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:06.670
Wanna do some training?

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:10.220
I work with dogs to train them
to go into an MRI scanner,

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:12.780
and try to figure out
what makes them tick, and

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:13.840
what they're thinking.

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:16.423
(bright music)

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:26.480
My name is Gregory Berns.

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:28.540
I'm a neuroscientist at Emory University

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:29.540
in Atlanta, Georgia.

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:34.170
I've grown up with dogs,
and I've lived with dogs

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:35.470
pretty much my whole life.

00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:39.180
One of my favorite dogs had passed away.

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:42.560
In the back of my mind I
started wondering, did that dog

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:44.969
love me in the way that I loved him?

00:00:44.969 --> 00:00:46.960
And that was really the beginning of it.

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:49.520
All of these basic things
that we've begun to

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:52.260
sort out in humans, at
least in human brains,

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:53.660
we want to do in dogs.

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:56.710
If dogs can be trained to
go into an MRI machine,

00:00:56.710 --> 00:00:58.150
then maybe we could figure out what

00:00:58.150 --> 00:00:59.720
they're actually thinking.

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:01.570
So, we have a dog in the scanner.

00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:05.410
That's Katie, and she is
a veteran on the project.

00:01:05.410 --> 00:01:08.786
This is probably about the
fifteenth time she's done this.

00:01:08.786 --> 00:01:13.780
(bright music)
(scanner blips)

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:17.130
Just having the idea of,
okay, well let's train dogs

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:20.550
to go on the scanner, it
wasn't immediately apparent

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:22.810
how you would go about doing that.

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:26.381
And MRI scanners are not the
most pleasant environments.

00:01:26.381 --> 00:01:29.410
(scanner blips)

00:01:29.410 --> 00:01:31.380
(directs dog)

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:32.320
Good girl!

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:35.890
Callie is the little black
terrier who was the first dog

00:01:35.890 --> 00:01:37.940
to train for the dog project.

00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:38.780
High five.

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:41.470
I built a simulator, oven MRI.

00:01:41.470 --> 00:01:45.480
So this is the simulator that
I built, here in my basement.

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:48.220
Then we started adding in
recordings of the scanner noise,

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:49.573
which is actually quite loud.

00:01:49.573 --> 00:01:50.910
(scanner blips)

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:53.450
We just started working
with, you know, treats

00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:55.630
and positive reinforcement
to see if we could get her

00:01:55.630 --> 00:01:56.840
to go into this thing.

00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:00.770
That actually proved to be pretty easy.

00:02:00.770 --> 00:02:03.360
And then so what we did is we
kind of put out word of mouth,

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:04.960
do you wanna join this
project, do you wanna train

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:07.380
your dogs for an MRI, and
you know, maybe figure out

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:08.213
what they're thinking?

00:02:08.213 --> 00:02:09.870
The purpose of this is
to go through a number

00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:12.740
of exercises including
basic obedience, as well as

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:14.960
how they react to the obstacles.

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:19.720
When we hold the trials, it
lets us weed out the dogs

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:22.740
that we don't think
would enjoy doing this.

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:25.930
One of the benefits then,
of having more participants

00:02:25.930 --> 00:02:27.600
is it gives you statistical power.

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:30.250
You can start to average
dogs brains together

00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:32.730
and get a better sense
for what's happening.

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:35.120
I started wondering very
seriously if we could

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:38.870
really finally answer this
question: do dogs essentially

00:02:38.870 --> 00:02:41.170
like us just for the social bond?

00:02:41.170 --> 00:02:42.390
Not about the food.

00:02:44.079 --> 00:02:46.920
And what we found was that in
looking at the reward system,

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:49.950
that almost all the dogs
had equal responses both

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:52.090
to food, as well as praise.

00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:54.420
Even a few dogs like the praise more.

00:02:56.310 --> 00:02:59.230
The things that we were
finding about the dogs brain,

00:02:59.230 --> 00:03:02.780
in many ways confirmed,
I think, what people

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:05.040
know in their hearts
about how dogs behave,

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:06.040
and why they behave.

00:03:08.211 --> 00:03:10.150
And the way I think about
dogs is in many ways

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:12.666
they're the ambassadors
to the animal world.

00:03:12.666 --> 00:03:14.079
(dog barks)

00:03:14.079 --> 00:03:16.040
They're not that
different from many of the

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:16.900
other mammals out there.

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:19.960
And so I suspect that a
lot of what we find in dogs

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:22.783
probably holds true for
pretty much any mammal.

00:03:22.783 --> 00:03:25.366
(gentle music)

